4?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/mjudeikis","id":3225744,"login":"mjudeikis","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjMyMjU3NDQ=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/railgrid/commits/e62339b3287d23cbf46796940f1444efbf8be93a/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-10-04T15:51:43Z","email":"mangirdas@judeikis.lt","name":"Mangirdas Judeikis"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-10-04T15:51:43Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Share the runner attempt lifecycle, and advertise what a runner''s host has (#782)\n\n* Share the runner attempt lifecycle, and advertise what a runner''s host has\n\nThe part of driving a runner that comes AFTER reaching one — following an\nattempt to the end, telling a park from a finish, reading a harness''s\nstream into text, tools and cost, answering a question or a permission\nprompt, stopping an attempt and seeing it stop — had been written twice,\nonce in the agents provider and once in Factory, and the two drifted. The\nagents copy learned that a terminal event can arrive before the terminal\nreceipt; Factory''s still believed the event. Factory refused a receipt\nwhose session id had changed; the agents copy knew a harness forks its\nsession on a resume. Each fix landed in one place and stayed there.\n\npkg/runner/dispatch is that lifecycle written once, next to the client\nthat reaches a runner. It holds the rules and nothing else: it persists\nnothing, decides nothing about who may answer a park, and holds no\ncredential past the call it was handed. A product plugs in an Observer\nfor the stream and keeps what is its own. The agents provider''s harness\nbackend is now an adapter over it; Factory follows in its own repository.\n\nTwo behaviours changed in the move, both on purpose:\n\n  - A run recovered after its process died used to answer a permission\n    prompt with \"deny\" on the grounds that nothing had been approved. It\n    now parks again with the same call: the person still gets to answer,\n    and a prompt nobody has seen is not refused on their behalf.\n  - Re-joining an attempt used to tail from cursor zero and stream the\n    whole conversation into the transcript a second time. It now tails\n    from the cursor the caller recorded. The receipt''s cursor is the\n    runner''s head, not what the caller consumed, and is never taken as\n    such.\n\nA supervised runner now advertises the toolchains it detects. A\nhand-written runner.json used to declare them; a supervised runner''s\nconfig is written by nobody, so it detected none, and a runner that\nclones with git while advertising no toolchains reported a machine that\ncould not do what it was doing. Factory refused it every job that needed\ngit. The agent resolves a short, named table the way the runner itself\nresolves a harness and advertises what it finds.\n\nThe edges provider projects that machine half of the capabilities\ndocument onto Service.status.runner — toolchains, environment,\nverification, capacity — beside the harness half already on\nstatus.harness, so a consumer matching a job to a machine reads the\npublished object and never probes the runner. The services schema name\nbumps accordingly.\n\nVerified: root, agents and edges modules build, vet and test; lint\nreports no issues; codegen is idempotent; the boilerplate, provider\ncontract and portalkit checks pass.\n\n* Make the runner ID optional in the shared runner client\n\nA caller that pins a runner by its Service — the object it reads and dials by\nname and UID — has no second identity to check it against: whichever runner\nthe edge published under that name is the one. Factory now works that way,\nsince its Worker kind is gone and an assignment freezes the Service instead.\n\nAn empty RunnerID therefore skips the reported-identity comparison and nothing\nelse: the protocol version is still checked, and every other identity in the\nenrollment is still required.\n\n* Let the coordinator name the snapshot commit''s subject\n\nEvery snapshot commit a runner exported said \"Implementation snapshot\", and\nCode verified exactly that, so a pull request revised three times showed\nthree identical commits. The fixed message exists so nothing a model wrote\ncan reach git metadata; it does not require the message to be meaningless.\n\nA start request may now carry commitMessage: one bounded, printable line of\ncoordinator-authored text — a ticket title, \"Address review on #7\". The\nrunner stamps exactly that (the canonical message when none is named), and\nthe coordinator hands the same text to Code with the snapshot, which verifies\nthe commit says it byte for byte. A commit saying anything else is refused\nas it always was.\n\n* Give Code a PullRequest resource\n\nA pull request on a managed repository is now a resource: what the forge\nshould show in the spec — the head branch, the base, the public text, and\nthe exact snapshot commit the branch should be at — and what it does show\nin the status: number, URL, head, state, merge, and the review conversation\nwith who wrote it and what kind of actor they are.\n\nThe reconciler does the least that closes the gap each pass. It advances\nthe head branch to the desired snapshot under an expected-head lease (the\nhead it last observed; an absent branch for the first push), so a branch\nthat moved under the coordinator is never overwritten; it opens the pull\nrequest once, adopting one a lost create answer left behind; and it re-reads\nan open pull request every minute. The staged bundle is the one the\nstage-snapshot verb filed, found by its content digest for the cluster.\n\nA coordinator that wants a pull request opened, revised or watched writes\nthis object and reads it. Nothing else of it needs a forge client.\n\n* Keep the remote''s branches current in a managed runner clone\n\nA follow-up on a pull request is based on the branch''s own head, which\nis reachable by commit alone, so the repository cache never had to fetch\nthe branches — and the agent''s checkout showed no main at all. Its\nsandbox cannot fetch for itself, so a reviewer asking it to reconcile\nthe branch with main got \"there is no conflict here to resolve\". The\nbranches are now refreshed on every attempt; a base the cache already\nholds still works with the remote unreachable, as it did.\n\n* Stop the PullRequest controller looping against the forge\n\nEvery reconcile of an open PullRequest wrote lastObserved into the\nstatus, and the controller watched its own status writes, so each pass\nre-enqueued the object at once. The one-minute requeue was never\nreached: an open pull request was re-read as fast as GitHub answered,\na dozen or more requests per pass, and one open pull request burned the\nwhole hourly rate budget in minutes. Every other Code reconciler on the\nsame credential then sat behind the shared rate-limit gate.\n\nOnly a spec change re-triggers the controller now. How often an open\npull request is re-read is the coordinator''s call, per line, because\nforges and review flows differ in how fast they move and what each read\ncosts: spec.observeInterval, five minutes when unset, never under\nthirty seconds.","tree":{"sha":"72d28258d55dc6f8e21c7b10842406146179961f","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/railgrid/git/trees/72d28258d55dc6f8e21c7b10842406146179961f"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/railgrid/git/commits/e62339b3287d23cbf46796940f1444efbf8be93a","verification":{"payload":"tree 72d28258d55dc6f8e21c7b10842406146179961f\nparent a909a0f07776669f0ed6cd6f6b9336208d345081\nauthor Mangirdas Judeikis <mangirdas@judeikis.lt> 1791129103 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1791129103 +0300\n\nShare the runner attempt lifecycle, and advertise what a runner''s host has (#782)\n\n* Share the runner attempt lifecycle, and advertise what a runner''s host has\n\nThe part of driving a runner that comes AFTER reaching one — following an\nattempt to the end, telling a park from a finish, reading a harness''s\nstream into text, tools and cost, answering a question or a permission\nprompt, stopping an attempt and seeing it stop — had been written twice,\nonce in the agents provider and once in Factory, and the two drifted. The\nagents copy learned that a terminal event can arrive before the terminal\nreceipt; Factory''s still believed the event. Factory refused a receipt\nwhose session id had changed; the agents copy knew a harness forks its\nsession on a resume. Each fix landed in one place and stayed there.\n\npkg/runner/dispatch is that lifecycle written once, next to the client\nthat reaches a runner. It holds the rules and nothing else: it persists\nnothing, decides nothing about who may answer a park, and holds no\ncredential past the call it was handed. A product plugs in an Observer\nfor the stream and keeps what is its own. The agents provider''s harness\nbackend is now an adapter over it; Factory follows in its own repository.\n\nTwo behaviours changed in the move, both on purpose:\n\n  - A run recovered after its process died used to answer a permission\n    prompt with \"deny\" on the grounds that nothing had been approved. It\n    now parks again with the same call: the person still gets to answer,\n    and a prompt nobody has seen is not refused on their behalf.\n  - Re-joining an attempt used to tail from cursor zero and stream the\n    whole conversation into the transcript a second time. It now tails\n    from the cursor the caller recorded. The receipt''s cursor is the\n    runner''s head, not what the caller consumed, and is never taken as\n    such.\n\nA supervised runner now advertises the toolchains it detects. A\nhand-written runner.json used to declare them; a supervised runner''s\nconfig is written by nobody, so it detected none, and a runner that\nclones with git while advertising no toolchains reported a machine that\ncould not do what it was doing. Factory refused it every job that needed\ngit. The agent resolves a short, named table the way the runner itself\nresolves a harness and advertises what it finds.\n\nThe edges provider projects that machine half of the capabilities\ndocument onto Service.status.runner — toolchains, environment,\nverification, capacity — beside the harness half already on\nstatus.harness, so a consumer matching a job to a machine reads the\npublished object and never probes the runner. The services schema name\nbumps accordingly.\n\nVerified: root, agents and edges modules build, vet and test; lint\nreports no issues; codegen is idempotent; the boilerplate, provider\ncontract and portalkit checks pass.\n\n* Make the runner ID optional in the shared runner client\n\nA caller that pins a runner by its Service — the object it reads and dials by\nname and UID — has no second identity to check it against: whichever runner\nthe edge published under that name is the one. Factory now works that way,\nsince its Worker kind is gone and an assignment freezes the Service instead.\n\nAn empty RunnerID therefore skips the reported-identity comparison and nothing\nelse: the protocol version is still checked, and every other identity in the\nenrollment is still required.\n\n* Let the coordinator name the snapshot commit''s subject\n\nEvery snapshot commit a runner exported said \"Implementation snapshot\", and\nCode verified exactly that, so a pull request revised three times showed\nthree identical commits. The fixed message exists so nothing a model wrote\ncan reach git metadata; it does not require the message to be meaningless.\n\nA start request may now carry commitMessage: one bounded, printable line of\ncoordinator-authored text — a ticket title, \"Address review on #7\". The\nrunner stamps exactly that (the canonical message when none is named), and\nthe coordinator hands the same text to Code with the snapshot, which verifies\nthe commit says it byte for byte. A commit saying anything else is refused\nas it always was.\n\n* Give Code a PullRequest resource\n\nA pull request on a managed repository is now a resource: what the forge\nshould show in the spec — the head branch, the base, the public text, and\nthe exact snapshot commit the branch should be at — and what it does show\nin the status: number, URL, head, state, merge, and the review conversation\nwith who wrote it and what kind of actor they are.\n\nThe reconciler does the least that closes the gap each pass. It advances\nthe head branch to the desired snapshot under an expected-head lease (the\nhead it last observed; an absent branch for the first push), so a branch\nthat moved under the coordinator is never overwritten; it opens the pull\nrequest once, adopting one a lost create answer left behind; and it re-reads\nan open pull request every minute. The staged bundle is the one the\nstage-snapshot verb filed, found by its content digest for the cluster.\n\nA coordinator that wants a pull request opened, revised or watched writes\nthis object and reads it. Nothing else of it needs a forge client.\n\n* Keep the remote''s branches current in a managed runner clone\n\nA follow-up on a pull request is based on the branch''s own head, which\nis reachable by commit alone, so the repository cache never had to fetch\nthe branches — and the agent''s checkout showed no main at all. Its\nsandbox cannot fetch for itself, so a reviewer asking it to reconcile\nthe branch with main got \"there is no conflict here to resolve\". The\nbranches are now refreshed on every attempt; a base the cache already\nholds still works with the remote unreachable, as it did.\n\n* Stop the PullRequest controller looping against the forge\n\nEvery reconcile of an open PullRequest wrote lastObserved into the\nstatus, and the controller watched its own status writes, so each pass\nre-enqueued the object at once. The one-minute requeue was never\nreached: an open pull request was re-read as fast as GitHub answered,\na dozen or more requests per pass, and one open pull request burned the\nwhole hourly rate budget in minutes. Every other Code reconciler on the\nsame credential then sat behind the shared rate-limit gate.\n\nOnly a spec change re-triggers the controller now. How often an open\npull request is re-read is the coordinator''s call, per line, because\nforges and review flows differ in how fast they move and what each read\ncosts: spec.observeInterval, five minutes when unset, never under\nthirty seconds.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqwnYPCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAxEwQAFQm1dBUB99yw9LmpuYWxbL0\nesDrAbnTEnN+Jr/g5wp3sqv0vFrILdRlPTnYDQ0jRO946Gll4/dTK2maU1yc8N5x\ntAJE2yAMyS2V/fBH5ri4oAoQ0fo8E/EDsjMduzEuK5uEKaeJJUzbNADsbKpg771u\nAp5+VqzmzOpSdqURWsTmroa2JgqIo59j5AZ/gFG1CQglA2orReoQywppJg6HpAMY\nxCDjDM8EKUgZ40dqePkgF4UrHUM4d+YoUH7FT4JzSZR+kjK/XlMhgVMImv9cLg3t\noTiKE9tXyc46jV6+HEIOftyC3feFRxG/amhnPXLFKfFlsCi7FR24PiFxTVEbCvoS\nrZ93CKS459voR4Ncz2fOChICZ0bUa7e7TUw9dmqzTqfNFxN3aaeyfZu9JZR/V5yQ\nuH+W/mDDu78a4Fy9SrOl5kkIjs0z5BD+BNpXNHHpUW21SoQ/Arr3C4kYF5WjAbAd\npBkxsWK27mESlI95frMlfaQwWDW/S9TVgmKa/xiWKCd9eHoaC1ojvsJUg8493nNx\nL/I/97hvmdJOIzi7nbpMQPN8fV99RFfVaIx2ZqN2uVoZdInghXDQPMrH3LFAYqPT\nbZ4YySXw+zltgWiBI7JKRai7IY/GOwWLQil6n87FionB0bNlG0nNxMVp1yk25vty\nw+rNFJ1ZofRRmeNXS2zv\n=Wzc3\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-10-04T15:51:44Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/railgrid/railgrid/commit/e62339b3287d23cbf46796940f1444efbf8be93a","node_id":"C_kwDORSHIc9oAKGU2MjMzOWIzMjg3ZDIzY2JmNDY3OTY5NDBmMTQ0NGVmYmY4YmU5M2E","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/railgrid/railgrid/commit/a909a0f07776669f0ed6cd6f6b9336208d345081","sha":"a909a0f07776669f0ed6cd6f6b9336208d345081","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/railgrid/commits/a909a0f07776669f0ed6cd6f6b9336208d345081"}],"sha":"e62339b3287d23cbf46796940f1444efbf8be93a","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/railgrid/commits/e62339b3287d23cbf46796940f1444efbf8be93a"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/3225744?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/mjudeikis","id":3225744,"login":"mjudeikis","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjMyMjU3NDQ=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/railgrid/commits/a909a0f07776669f0ed6cd6f6b9336208d345081/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-10-03T05:38:02Z","email":"mangirdas@judeikis.lt","name":"Mangirdas Judeikis"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-10-03T05:38:02Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Take the upstream fixes for four new govulncheck findings (#783)\n\n* Take the upstream fixes for four new govulncheck findings\n\nThe vulnerability database gained four entries the gate reaches from this\ntree, and each has a fix to take rather than an exposure to accept:\n\n  GO-2026-6497, GO-2026-6499  oras.land/oras-go/v2            2.6.1 -> 2.6.2\n  GO-2026-6505                otel otlptrace exporters         1.44  -> 1.45\n  GO-2026-6597                github.com/containerd/containerd 1.7.35 -> 1.7.36\n\nThe otel one needs the grpc and http exporter modules bumped as well as the\nparent: they are separate modules and the advisory names all three. Root and\nproviders/edges carry them; provider-sdk does not. Both gates pass locally.\n\n* Carry the same fixes into every module that links the affected packages\n\nThe vulnerability database is read live, and the first pass bumped only the\ntwo modules the gate had failed on by then. Every module that links the\notel trace exporters needs the same pin — provider-sdk directly, and\nquickstart through its provider-sdk replace, where otel itself had to follow\nto 1.45 for the exporters'' semconv. The modules that replace the root\nmodule (agents) only needed their go.sum brought up to date with the root''s\nnew graph; without that the agents image stopped building.\n\nEvery module builds and every govulncheck gate passes locally.","tree":{"sha":"f7b5714d7ceda7da3cc414e1ef1977d6ee5e90af","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/railgrid/git/trees/f7b5714d7ceda7da3cc414e1ef1977d6ee5e90af"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/railgrid/git/commits/a909a0f07776669f0ed6cd6f6b9336208d345081","verification":{"payload":"tree f7b5714d7ceda7da3cc414e1ef1977d6ee5e90af\nparent 91de94e23fa5f5bf7ff66c6aa375b535d586cc3d\nauthor Mangirdas Judeikis <mangirdas@judeikis.lt> 1791005882 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1791005882 +0300\n\nTake the upstream fixes for four new govulncheck findings (#783)\n\n* Take the upstream fixes for four new govulncheck findings\n\nThe vulnerability database gained four entries the gate reaches from this\ntree, and each has a fix to take rather than an exposure to accept:\n\n  GO-2026-6497, GO-2026-6499  oras.land/oras-go/v2            2.6.1 -> 2.6.2\n  GO-2026-6505                otel otlptrace exporters         1.44  -> 1.45\n  GO-2026-6597                github.com/containerd/containerd 1.7.35 -> 1.7.36\n\nThe otel one needs the grpc and http exporter modules bumped as well as the\nparent: they are separate modules and the advisory names all three. Root and\nproviders/edges carry them; provider-sdk does not. Both gates pass locally.\n\n* Carry the same fixes into every module that links the affected packages\n\nThe vulnerability database is read live, and the first pass bumped only the\ntwo modules the gate had failed on by then. Every module that links the\notel trace exporters needs the same pin — provider-sdk directly, and\nquickstart through its provider-sdk replace, where otel itself had to follow\nto 1.45 for the exporters'' semconv. The modules that replace the root\nmodule (agents) only needed their go.sum brought up to date with the root''s\nnew graph; without that the agents image stopped building.\n\nEvery module builds and every govulncheck gate passes locally.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqwJS6CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAYykQAErQd5rVShPxsBlVbr7El+Kw\nufjc19JBS3EkLT6q4SKKxRMjR59Y7awO6DhOvIpX82CCqmG3Mh802xdyhvbN5UhH\nmL8OS3yhDLCoa9Q1fmYvsnrIFrxv63LKFKFHbNlRyZ7lynjLSIWRLNMjG04pymho\nKwAQW8KftEBsntWdEibzOjFYTPtqO+NxkFXdKFoFlqACwvK/3WL0rsdhlM8vc3B4\nIDol8AGyqK3uv167cymvo+MxcgZb57kTgRW6N7X1mLtw91SFLNiMUm8kY4DwH4cP\nS8NQezOETAhEPWqQWSlC+Hgq1CMWPTS6SoP7Mdyj5fihovQ758RCBpztCz98LhBQ\nbZ0FKWs6q2Ku02bvKWcclU1LbVkaCiBl0OtDPfLnsQlAtd7p1BfcwKUYWhZ/YV5B\nZNbFINHDw+wS9i9ZO1o4CM47BYDEo50jevXsUg1U0f//1692q37xLFS3gT5MBusc\niM27C0cTsWHx9VhNUCVokcWzH5P2tdWBqZq/d33H60/40HFUmKuMLmgzUt7tZK46\nxGjcDZ4Eup3C1I70QkIANZAriCjdtoGVX42gS/x6iuHFkmqMBmK3sH0TyvhEF8PR\nIXtZaBjiWzgfU+ZsKft3/ZLmBb2zreCS/nxmSdPOqWDXDFOlXBH+CpyJ5mS/eJFr\nZ02j5iYYx73QA6tSibNa\n=ykAF\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-10-03T05:38:03Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/railgrid/railgrid/commit/a909a0f07776669f0ed6cd6f6b9336208d345081","node_id":"C_kwDORSHIc9oAKGE5MDlhMGYwNzc3NjY2OWYwZWQ2Y2Q2ZjZiOTMzNjIwOGQzNDUwODE","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/railgrid/railgrid/commit/91de94e23fa5f5bf7ff66c6aa375b535d586cc3d","sha":"91de94e23fa5f5bf7ff66c6aa375b535d586cc3d","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/railgrid/commits/91de94e23fa5f5bf7ff66c6aa375b535d586cc3d"}],"sha":"a909a0f07776669f0ed6cd6f6b9336208d345081","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/railgrid/commits/a909a0f07776669f0ed6cd6f6b9336208d345081"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/3225744?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/mjudeikis","id":3225744,"login":"mjudeikis","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjMyMjU3NDQ=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/railgrid/commits/91de94e23fa5f5bf7ff66c6aa375b535d586cc3d/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-29T10:05:03Z","email":"mangirdas@judeikis.lt","name":"Mangirdas Judeikis"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-29T10:05:03Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Serve the embedded CatalogEntry when none is mounted (#781)\n\nA verb is reached only as a kcp custom subresource, so serve derives its\nsubresource table from the CatalogEntry manifest and refuses to start\nwithout one. Only the chart''s init-container path mounts that file\n(RAILGRID_CATALOGENTRY_FILE); the image bakes deploy/chart/files, which\ndoes not contain the templated CatalogEntry.\n\nIn operator mode the serve Deployment the operator creates mounts\nnothing, so every serve pod crash-looped with \"no CatalogEntry manifest\nfound\" once bootstrap started succeeding.\n\nFall back to the manifest already embedded in the binary — the same\nbytes the operator applies to the provider workspace — so the declared\ncoordinates are a property of the build, not of the install. A mounted\nfile still wins.","tree":{"sha":"e6e20e2c736d9a41fb462daa0b3e334efd771433","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/railgrid/git/trees/e6e20e2c736d9a41fb462daa0b3e334efd771433"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/railgrid/git/commits/91de94e23fa5f5bf7ff66c6aa375b535d586cc3d","verification":{"payload":"tree e6e20e2c736d9a41fb462daa0b3e334efd771433\nparent d3cdcfc2405a657dff96b51c75061461fe2b75a6\nauthor Mangirdas Judeikis <mangirdas@judeikis.lt> 1790676303 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790676303 +0300\n\nServe the embedded CatalogEntry when none is mounted (#781)\n\nA verb is reached only as a kcp custom subresource, so serve derives its\nsubresource table from the CatalogEntry manifest and refuses to start\nwithout one. Only the chart''s init-container path mounts that file\n(RAILGRID_CATALOGENTRY_FILE); the image bakes deploy/chart/files, which\ndoes not contain the templated CatalogEntry.\n\nIn operator mode the serve Deployment the operator creates mounts\nnothing, so every serve pod crash-looped with \"no CatalogEntry manifest\nfound\" once bootstrap started succeeding.\n\nFall back to the manifest already embedded in the binary — the same\nbytes the operator applies to the provider workspace — so the declared\ncoordinates are a property of the build, not of the install. A mounted\nfile still wins.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqu41PCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAw+cQAK4DUmzbtnkM2fVyAk+bnwsV\nivI/nXEXkcDpG3GdA9Y6O6/DEvmkBrvOQFcVZ8KFqfkK2qShQWFw1awEdKUDs3x3\nNKusIVNPMu97WB9QHJV1Tra7AsXLrpCh2BRiI020DiNJf0Wxc0LeDEPW+09nHsYd\nPq5Pi7q7Hm3t14L2YOBkRC5QYEnx9rReUnEVUeRBZneMp1W/SvplPpe5bw4d+rn+\nM2COxq3nS/0e1y7na2cSml/JZQev2cfdlCBjFHQj3VGTBeZMXvuKtJmYKxROsPJE\n1567/1aNOmRlpB1iZRD1AqtclF17XPeb9AfRL6WM5dWw4X9sjjznQMqNjkHL7W/q\nO+ydx7e17RBTenip+Wmt7ZKA02+KpuN0wus/SboJcIPOFxvNEOmWrEm1s1rHpZil\nu8pHYaktvNV3FBR3r5R+Viwo8JqG7rilzkDCQ8cM00o5ZYZupCZ9WYvgkXZ49awm\noSVYuZA3TjJenayPh/ov44AixZTPkGpGlstdYQ/u2eWW+T5wUn6PG5ed10KHc2uI\nF503zFbxyh7Go7kXokmbKbnZEkt9PPtE7ISKOJYjMgGTjTe6R+HSAwLr+ZNwLvF7\nrJ6rJLOBSa+GEXKOF+8CTZfOhBbZJvz9FngRnjWWWanpgxSHiwuEA95p+K5XJA0B\nxJSXaJOtZTDdIf4glTu1\n=xBQ0\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-29T10:05:03Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/railgrid/railgrid/commit/91de94e23fa5f5bf7ff66c6aa375b535d586cc3d","node_id":"C_kwDORSHIc9oAKDkxZGU5NGUyM2ZhNWY1YmY3ZmY2NmM2YWEzNzViNTM1ZDU4NmNjM2Q","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/railgrid/railgrid/commit/d3cdcfc2405a657dff96b51c75061461fe2b75a6","sha":"d3cdcfc2405a657dff96b51c75061461fe2b75a6","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/railgrid/commits/d3cdcfc2405a657dff96b51c75061461fe2b75a6"}],"sha":"91de94e23fa5f5bf7ff66c6aa375b535d586cc3d","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/railgrid/commits/91de94e23fa5f5bf7ff66c6aa375b535d586cc3d"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/3225744?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/mjudeikis","id":3225744,"login":"mjudeikis","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjMyMjU3NDQ=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/railgrid/commits/d3cdcfc2405a657dff96b51c75061461fe2b75a6/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-29T09:37:39Z","email":"mangirdas@judeikis.lt","name":"Mangirdas Judeikis"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-29T09:37:39Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Give operator-mode bootstrap a data-plane URL (#780)\n\nThe infrastructure APIExport declares instances/<verb> subresources, so\nsdkinstall.Bootstrap publishes a DataPlaneEndpointSlice with the address\nkcp reverse-proxies those requests to. It takes that address from\nOptions.DataPlaneURL, or from the CatalogEntry named by\nOptions.CatalogEntryFile.\n\nThe CRD operator passed neither, and the image bakes deploy/chart/files,\nwhich does not contain the templated CatalogEntry — so catalogEntryPath()\nresolves to nothing in operator mode. Every bootstrap on a fresh provider\nworkspace failed with \"DataPlaneEndpointSlice: no data-plane base URL\",\nleaving the provider unbootstrapped.\n\nDerive the URL from the serve Service the operator itself creates\n(ServeBaseURL), which is the same address the chart''s CatalogEntry\npublishes.","tree":{"sha":"9711b9b343967edf382b48f948c6ee2954473c38","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/railgrid/git/trees/9711b9b343967edf382b48f948c6ee2954473c38"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/railgrid/git/commits/d3cdcfc2405a657dff96b51c75061461fe2b75a6","verification":{"payload":"tree 9711b9b343967edf382b48f948c6ee2954473c38\nparent ee564053639a7dc5d60f0d7d953856627c3cfab0\nauthor Mangirdas Judeikis <mangirdas@judeikis.lt> 1790674659 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790674659 +0300\n\nGive operator-mode bootstrap a data-plane URL (#780)\n\nThe infrastructure APIExport declares instances/<verb> subresources, so\nsdkinstall.Bootstrap publishes a DataPlaneEndpointSlice with the address\nkcp reverse-proxies those requests to. It takes that address from\nOptions.DataPlaneURL, or from the CatalogEntry named by\nOptions.CatalogEntryFile.\n\nThe CRD operator passed neither, and the image bakes deploy/chart/files,\nwhich does not contain the templated CatalogEntry — so catalogEntryPath()\nresolves to nothing in operator mode. Every bootstrap on a fresh provider\nworkspace failed with \"DataPlaneEndpointSlice: no data-plane base URL\",\nleaving the provider unbootstrapped.\n\nDerive the URL from the serve Service the operator itself creates\n(ServeBaseURL), which is the same address the chart''s CatalogEntry\npublishes.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqu4bjCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAVDAQABfOZwyiwM/nNthG0wvU+bjV\nvHs4Cwx0aVoSo0NRGi8bdITX8yg4iJSjj1AGr4SBxFOtyzXQNXjOgJ7+4bHRH/kz\nggrnAhnM5/82UMlvjTxq0WWi4PhZafs4OAUeDV5Fps6DYS5dGUXWTijiRuItgCsZ\n0sqJSFizoj7CTRwQbPrnlo0enCgoiiQKEgcV65Eim45+XVLdLaGAM0FcJU8DrPaj\n8vyrDXWNEwAVuhJTgIlHJuZvOzuq0RZzehMndlaJSdY8iDplBntfiW4Jl+DI1j2e\n8Fty3AvWpoW/sSk3Bljm4msqk2m5zCr17zE49+rcdHTN34pg4yJTaNjwTF36oKdg\nCcXl6Vizqs/3OPJXKzWktG4k7gVP/c/d1A44azoPJxg85qY6PKBQv8ROi1xh0Tom\nudYEYA9q/P7P3XJr9pTx7VDU+ES3Zt25B+zBMpf65X/b5cSoJku9hNB6zrbEi5Sy\nzq7g9wJZJX/aRYbDUOPXBBIufSwhLCaeynAX9pTHjFRp9/z+f3Jxfp7AXAUXjxL2\noC3kxo7Y6kkZAmue/A6kaQQJR/lFIlFY6lQaF2YeJCN56QxWSLNzhNz/LtdOpTVk\nDIov9OOxsk4EJAlBuzeZH16+Dt2eFPaEfcA/2axA18QVj0eiwyyAkZ3FT3hIWiKy\nNFfkla7XYtJIL4xpMMb9\n=gpkG\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-29T09:37:39Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/railgrid/railgrid/commit/d3cdcfc2405a657dff96b51c75061461fe2b75a6","node_id":"C_kwDORSHIc9oAKGQzY2RjZmMyNDA1YTY1N2RmZjk2YjUxYzc1MDYxNDYxZmUyYjc1YTY","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/railgrid/railgrid/commit/ee564053639a7dc5d60f0d7d953856627c3cfab0","sha":"ee564053639a7dc5d60f0d7d953856627c3cfab0","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/railgrid/commits/ee564053639a7dc5d60f0d7d953856627c3cfab0"}],"sha":"d3cdcfc2405a657dff96b51c75061461fe2b75a6","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/railgrid/commits/d3cdcfc2405a657dff96b51c75061461fe2b75a6"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/3225744?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/mjudeikis","id":3225744,"login":"mjudeikis","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjMyMjU3NDQ=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/railgrid/commits/ee564053639a7dc5d60f0d7d953856627c3cfab0/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-29T06:21:26Z","email":"mangirdas@judeikis.lt","name":"Mangirdas Judeikis"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-29T06:21:26Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Back an agent with a coding harness on an edge (#779)\n\nAn Agent could only ever be a hub-side chat loop against an\nOpenAI-compatible endpoint. The machinery to run Claude Code or Codex on\nan edge existed end to end but was reachable only by Factory, gated\nbehind a tenant-declared Addon, a second credential path and a client\nprivate to that repo.\n\nspec.backend now says WHERE an agent''s turns execute: \"model\" (today''s\nin-process loop) or \"harness\" (a coding harness on one of the\nworkspace''s machines). The provider owns runs, transcripts, budgets,\nchannels and approvals in both cases; the backend owns the turn.\n\nThe edge is the harness provider. spec.harness on LinuxServer and\nMacOSServer decides which harnesses a machine offers, defaulting to\nauto — every harness installed on it — so a joined machine with Claude\nCode on it is usable without a second step. The agent applies a change\nlive, caches it on disk for an offline start, and install flags only\nseed it. It supervises one runner per harness on a loopback port,\ninjects the runner''s bearer itself so no credential enters the\nworkspace, and advertises each runner over the discovery channel the\nseventeen other service types already use. The edges provider publishes\nit as an ordinary Service of the new type \"runner\".\n\nThe caller brings the credential. A ModelCredential of provider\nclaude-code or codex is sent with every attempt as dispatch data, the\nway a clone token already is: stripped before fingerprinting, held in\nmemory for one attempt, never in the journal, an event or a log. A\nrunner has no identity of its own, so a machine that hosts a harness is\nnot a shared billable account. Start refuses an attempt without one.\n\nA request the permission mode does not pre-approve now stops at a human\ninstead of being denied silently. The runner hosts a one-tool MCP server\nfor its own child; the call parks the attempt, the verdict resumes the\nsame turn, and deny is an answer the harness can respond to rather than\na failed run. spec.harness.permissionMode and .allowedTools are the\nmachine''s ceiling, set by whoever owns the machine, and a caller cannot\nraise them.\n\nDeleted: the Addon kind, its controller, the addon-credentials verb and\nthe whole host-side credential path; Factory''s two private packages, now\npkg/runner/client; the agents provider''s duplicated resume lifecycle and\nits in-memory executor queue, because the Run object is the queue.\n\nrunner/v1 stays runner/v1. Every protocol addition is optional and\nFactory''s requests remain valid.\n\nPre-existing bugs fixed along the way:\n\n  - verifyTaskPath and artifactSource compared a resolved path against an\n    unresolved root, so a runner whose state directory sat under a\n    symlink refused every attempt and every artifact.\n  - the shared client turned any failed response carrying a protocol code\n    into a protocol error, discarding the status, so a 401 or 403 whose\n    body said \"attempt not found\" read as the one answer that permits a\n    first start.\n  - the engine applied the caller''s approval verdict to the first tool\n    call of a recovery resume, whose checkpoint has no pending call.\n  - the MCPServer role dropped the addons resource wholesale; the same\n    privilege now lives on the edge spec, so the denial is verb-aware.\n  - deleting the worker pool removed the only ceiling on concurrent runs;\n    a non-blocking slot ceiling hands a run back to the queue instead.\n\nVerified: both provider modules and the root module build, vet and test\nclean; codegen is idempotent; the provider contract reports no\nviolations; and the edges connectivity e2e suite proves an SSE stream\nsurvives the Service proxy frame by frame.","tree":{"sha":"e564d12bf6fb6fc29a2ef2b0a17841af2458cedd","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/railgrid/git/trees/e564d12bf6fb6fc29a2ef2b0a17841af2458cedd"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/railgrid/git/commits/ee564053639a7dc5d60f0d7d953856627c3cfab0","verification":{"payload":"tree e564d12bf6fb6fc29a2ef2b0a17841af2458cedd\nparent 236bce59c3ad09fb1945bb2acbd80ca7777ae02f\nauthor Mangirdas Judeikis <mangirdas@judeikis.lt> 1790662886 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790662886 +0300\n\nBack an agent with a coding harness on an edge (#779)\n\nAn Agent could only ever be a hub-side chat loop against an\nOpenAI-compatible endpoint. The machinery to run Claude Code or Codex on\nan edge existed end to end but was reachable only by Factory, gated\nbehind a tenant-declared Addon, a second credential path and a client\nprivate to that repo.\n\nspec.backend now says WHERE an agent''s turns execute: \"model\" (today''s\nin-process loop) or \"harness\" (a coding harness on one of the\nworkspace''s machines). The provider owns runs, transcripts, budgets,\nchannels and approvals in both cases; the backend owns the turn.\n\nThe edge is the harness provider. spec.harness on LinuxServer and\nMacOSServer decides which harnesses a machine offers, defaulting to\nauto — every harness installed on it — so a joined machine with Claude\nCode on it is usable without a second step. The agent applies a change\nlive, caches it on disk for an offline start, and install flags only\nseed it. It supervises one runner per harness on a loopback port,\ninjects the runner''s bearer itself so no credential enters the\nworkspace, and advertises each runner over the discovery channel the\nseventeen other service types already use. The edges provider publishes\nit as an ordinary Service of the new type \"runner\".\n\nThe caller brings the credential. A ModelCredential of provider\nclaude-code or codex is sent with every attempt as dispatch data, the\nway a clone token already is: stripped before fingerprinting, held in\nmemory for one attempt, never in the journal, an event or a log. A\nrunner has no identity of its own, so a machine that hosts a harness is\nnot a shared billable account. Start refuses an attempt without one.\n\nA request the permission mode does not pre-approve now stops at a human\ninstead of being denied silently. The runner hosts a one-tool MCP server\nfor its own child; the call parks the attempt, the verdict resumes the\nsame turn, and deny is an answer the harness can respond to rather than\na failed run. spec.harness.permissionMode and .allowedTools are the\nmachine''s ceiling, set by whoever owns the machine, and a caller cannot\nraise them.\n\nDeleted: the Addon kind, its controller, the addon-credentials verb and\nthe whole host-side credential path; Factory''s two private packages, now\npkg/runner/client; the agents provider''s duplicated resume lifecycle and\nits in-memory executor queue, because the Run object is the queue.\n\nrunner/v1 stays runner/v1. Every protocol addition is optional and\nFactory''s requests remain valid.\n\nPre-existing bugs fixed along the way:\n\n  - verifyTaskPath and artifactSource compared a resolved path against an\n    unresolved root, so a runner whose state directory sat under a\n    symlink refused every attempt and every artifact.\n  - the shared client turned any failed response carrying a protocol code\n    into a protocol error, discarding the status, so a 401 or 403 whose\n    body said \"attempt not found\" read as the one answer that permits a\n    first start.\n  - the engine applied the caller''s approval verdict to the first tool\n    call of a recovery resume, whose checkpoint has no pending call.\n  - the MCPServer role dropped the addons resource wholesale; the same\n    privilege now lives on the edge spec, so the denial is verb-aware.\n  - deleting the worker pool removed the only ceiling on concurrent runs;\n    a non-blocking slot ceiling hands a run back to the queue instead.\n\nVerified: both provider modules and the root module build, vet and test\nclean; codegen is idempotent; the provider contract reports no\nviolations; and the edges connectivity e2e suite proves an SSE stream\nsurvives the Service proxy frame by frame.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqu1jmCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAk2sQAIexU1y/s43oIw8Dx/CqEH8R\n0o7iLwNbbXISH4FfrTaliNEvAMoIJzk8UlIV24lBdYq6HBP2qYPyAfAwais7pBZo\nnHDK19TAOBZvmk1QZ0z7WNrKqtomR5Scfm8gXHb1gZjbczaNINRrEgBa0FdK1sdO\nEfj6QdOce0QzWF2x9AnIs1dmdIrn6hXdGACIkM1YI3gvVyTNC/kS51IK2qZRrz0A\nxXQHoTnzZprE4C7iB72E7LEoWKPy+J6wKVjmZ1r9sGqPiPLvrHX59RVZ7PXmIBH2\nnQVpFCgA5E9DCvWdyZLZXpRUI6ZnBOKnwR72U//Eu87p3z+pDcoNP+gCigss3pLH\nRKJ+iaY3WWafFZ4UXqmGfYJ5b9O/x7Tdn/eFlJWR+21sVlsy7jVkx+q1LNdk2/jj\nI79WMKizPHx8t3BCo84oU9qPOWY29O8/nRd6iZbKHOJJVFgJefzuz8mVJeLJT6d8\nRaLSIbUy4Xy/CdPnU/IGnNLEdihFzBHvGiph8KNQb0/Un+Wy1GDtBaS8xxn6GBsE\nD7I3JoM4cImWyRj59AlvY+zIyEx3OP+nnvLCCIhNstAy1exx91np8fR6rTqfbZv4\nwUya2izS+elzFn4umS8Og0anTOIaOP6Ym8e30Rbk1Pi46asc4rfYUSE+4HxyxnxY\nzp7R1PX2f8gaJhHGv9XQ\n=07JI\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-29T06:21:27Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/railgrid/railgrid/commit/ee564053639a7dc5d60f0d7d953856627c3cfab0","node_id":"C_kwDORSHIc9oAKGVlNTY0MDUzNjM5YTdkYzVkNjBmMGQ3ZDk1Mzg1NjYyN2MzY2ZhYjA","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/railgrid/railgrid/commit/236bce59c3ad09fb1945bb2acbd80ca7777ae02f","sha":"236bce59c3ad09fb1945bb2acbd80ca7777ae02f","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/railgrid/commits/236bce59c3ad09fb1945bb2acbd80ca7777ae02f"}],"sha":"ee564053639a7dc5d60f0d7d953856627c3cfab0","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/railgrid/commits/ee564053639a7dc5d60f0d7d953856627c3cfab0"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/5685031?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cwilhit/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cwilhit/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cwilhit/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cwilhit/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/cwilhit","id":5685031,"login":"cwilhit","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjU2ODUwMzE=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cwilhit/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cwilhit/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cwilhit/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cwilhit/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cwilhit/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cwilhit","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/railgrid/commits/236bce59c3ad09fb1945bb2acbd80ca7777ae02f/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-26T14:23:12Z","email":"cdwilhite0601@gmail.com","name":"Craig D Wilhite"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-26T14:23:12Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Codex/semantic bi agent experience (#778)\n\n* fix(portalkit): preserve resource header action layout\n\n* feat(agents): add inline charts and durable conversation history","tree":{"sha":"85340f3f646fb1c88246088b7a496c06ced76852","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/railgrid/git/trees/85340f3f646fb1c88246088b7a496c06ced76852"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/railgrid/git/commits/236bce59c3ad09fb1945bb2acbd80ca7777ae02f","verification":{"payload":"tree 85340f3f646fb1c88246088b7a496c06ced76852\nparent 76b9080bce003fec7a1d8b66324373a791842149\nauthor Craig D Wilhite <cdwilhite0601@gmail.com> 1790432592 -0400\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790432592 -0400\n\nCodex/semantic bi agent experience (#778)\n\n* fix(portalkit): preserve resource header action layout\n\n* feat(agents): add inline charts and durable conversation history","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqt9VQCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAqHoQAJG6Mh5E9biMVmrR6Go9QFv+\n8tHgW6MiXsCEcJQf5YwVYAVSLzoAH2PYfALWAKUeQzvMlQRqAL/RCpqZJ/Vkx2SP\nBxOfDX6FPQrSFWvkcHYsbWCoaa8ZBU54Wi2/yYiUWIGg8v/mvHmWxFTUbeItFg5w\nHGdo7TBVnRaU1kFIhOpmqVQRkf5ndhLroGjYs+/WoLmgokxZukc0jkKxMijxdQ4T\nOuX6wFqSxUmGePHn1D2+wujI/e3claRA1g3viNzG/yn241sX/fglsydeIlyfILCd\nQc97RjfUVhavRUeUdfdp5f+jCyc2vTZ6Sr1V6ip0juSVeg9N+9fF8D7QC3r1BLz5\ndzWKlO2GQbJ0p2HAUW943EdigXV82ojFxLp39On6I+QjqKfy7CM5QjVwkwTzonl6\n6X5DUw4PEhwLJIJ0RleD4nhR+pUz4oPYT5+HEkXcQKSjRflpZ5WL5NqkaiAd/tAd\nmPK+t8Rx9ZHi/cnnaM9qnhq5zbM5TKQ3A8MGQeKQEbmuzZKnFs7W3aAFAm6+/UGe\nnpeyCGXao/d3PBhWqmC/afl1yufu/MTCDSS1aGR5Jcul1eHamwCyYnRS7DoTVe7R\nJBQgayPvh5lDO7O4hpV8KL7frpW+MXHb7/zhKfwZGsdqqpazq5HxzFA4GTiti8Uc\nlBTZwPRElahLILmRSyKI\n=ORlm\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-26T14:23:12Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/railgrid/railgrid/commit/236bce59c3ad09fb1945bb2acbd80ca7777ae02f","node_id":"C_kwDORSHIc9oAKDIzNmJjZTU5YzNhZDA5ZmIxOTQ1YmIyYWNiZDgwY2E3Nzc3YWUwMmY","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/railgrid/railgrid/commit/76b9080bce003fec7a1d8b66324373a791842149","sha":"76b9080bce003fec7a1d8b66324373a791842149","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/railgrid/commits/76b9080bce003fec7a1d8b66324373a791842149"}],"sha":"236bce59c3ad09fb1945bb2acbd80ca7777ae02f","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/railgrid/commits/236bce59c3ad09fb1945bb2acbd80ca7777ae02f"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/3225744?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/mjudeikis","id":3225744,"login":"mjudeikis","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjMyMjU3NDQ=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/railgrid/commits/76b9080bce003fec7a1d8b66324373a791842149/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-26T08:37:11Z","email":"mangirdas@judeikis.lt","name":"Mangirdas Judeikis"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-26T08:37:11Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Adjust providers machinery (#777)\n\n* Fix nerw provider struct\n\n* Adjust providers logic\n\n* Move to better providers machinery\n\n* move provider contract\n\n* fix CI\n\n* Claim the kind a required verb coordinate hangs off\n\nkcp refuses an APIExport whose permission claim names a custom\nsubresource without a claim on the resource that serves it. App Studio\nrequired code.railgrid.ai connections/mint-registry-token but never\nconnections, so `init` died at admission:\n\n  spec.permissionClaims[13].resource: Invalid value:\n  \"connections/mint-registry-token\": a claim on a custom subresource\n  requires a claim on \"connections\", which is what serves it\n\nApp Studio does read those Connections — it lists them when a project\npicks one and gets one back when a view resolves its connectionRef — so\nthe claim was simply missing, not deliberately absent. Declare it\nread-only and regenerate the export.\n\nValidateProviderRequirement now rejects a verb coordinate whose parent is\nnot declared in the same requirement, in either order, so the next one\nfails in `make verify` with the coordinate named rather than at a\nprovider''s own bootstrap.\n\nThe infrastructure provider e2e reported \"provider init failed: exit\nstatus 1\" and left the reason in a temp file CI does not upload; tail it\nto stderr.\n\nAgents ran with the WRONG identity: run-provider-agents resolved\nRAILGRID_PROVIDER_KUBECONFIG by taking the first file that existed out of\nthe provider''s own kubeconfig, kcp''s admin kubeconfig and the Tilt front\nproxy''s. Started before `agents-init` mints the first, it silently bound\nto kcp-admin, which authenticates but holds nothing in the provider\nworkspace, so every data-plane verb failed the gate:\n\n  dataplane: reading APIExportEndpointSlice agents.railgrid.ai:\n  Unauthorized\n\nserving HTTP 500 to the portal''s model discovery, and the controllers\nlogged Unauthorized lease errors on a loop. A verb runs as the provider,\nso no other identity will do: use the provider''s own kubeconfig or refuse\nto start, and watch the file in Tilt so init''s write restarts serve —\nwhat kuery and infrastructure already do.\n\n* Accept every requirement kuery declares when enabling it\n\nTestKueryAggregatesEdgeObjects failed with kuery unable to discover the\nedge''s Kubernetes API:\n\n  kuery engage: discovery failed for cluster 1vqly0gm36vbqs0b/kuery-k8s:\n  unknown\n\nThe edges provider never saw the request. kcp refused it first:\n\n  authorization step reason=\"failed to find suitable reason to allow\n  access in APIBinding\" decision=\"Denied\" resource=\"kubernetesclusters\"\n  subresource=\"k8s\" verb=\"get\" ... resp=403\n\nThe tenant''s APIBinding asked for three claims and accepted one. Its\nexportPermissionClaims carried kubernetesclusters, kubernetesclusters/k8s\nand subjectaccessreviews; appliedPermissionClaims carried only\nkubernetesclusters, because the test''s Enable ticked that one kind by\nname. A claim the binding does not accept is a coordinate the virtual\nworkspace does not serve, so the k8s verb was never reachable and the\ndiscovery client rendered the 403 as \"unknown\".\n\nRead the declaration from the hub catalog and accept all of it, which is\nwhat the portal and `railgrid dev` already do. A hardcoded list stops\nmatching spec.requires the moment a requirement is added, which is how\nthis test came to enable a kind without the verb on it.\n\n* Read the e2e bootstrap logs before cleanup deletes them\n\nThe tail added for the infrastructure provider e2e ran after cleanup(),\nwhich removes the whole data dir, so the failure reported\n\n  provider init failed: exit status 1 (log: /tmp/.../init.log)\n  (open /tmp/.../init.log: no such file or directory)\n\nTake the tail first. Give the serve readiness check the same treatment:\nits log is in the same directory and would be just as gone.\n\n* Claim the infrastructure Templates App Studio reads\n\nProject details failed with:\n\n  templates.infrastructure.railgrid.ai is forbidden: User\n  \"system:serviceaccount:default:provider\" cannot list resource\n  \"templates\" in API group \"infrastructure.railgrid.ai\" at the cluster\n  scope: access denied\n\nApp Studio reads that catalog in three places — preparing a project''s\ndetail view, resolving the template a binding names, and the assistant''s\ntemplate tool — but spec.requires named only instances and its verb\ncoordinates, so the generated claim never covered the kind and kcp served\nnothing for it. Declare it read-only.\n\nAudited every provider for the same class both ways a GVR is spelled in\nthis tree, the composite literal and GroupVersion.WithResource: no other\nkind is read from a group its provider does not declare. The one other\ncross-group read, agents reading the workspace''s MCPServer, is deliberate\n— it runs as the CALLER, on their bearer, so it needs no claim.\n\n* Revendor kcp at the custom-subresource parent gate\n\nmjudeikis/kcp 3b4bd7a70 (\"fix getter\"), one commit on from ba9e0a6a4.\nIt adds gateOnParent to the APIExport virtual workspace''s custom\nsubresource storage: before a verb runs, the object it hangs off is read\nback through the storage that serves the parent, label selector and all,\nso a claimer cannot reach a subresource of an object its claim does not\ncover.\n\nThat makes the parent claim load-bearing at request time rather than only\nat APIExport admission. Every verb coordinate this repo declares already\nsits under a claimed parent, including the code.railgrid.ai/connections\none added earlier in this branch, so the gate has an object to read in\neach case.\n\nAll twelve pins move together: the six replace targets in the root module\nplus the apimachinery/v2 pin the SDK and four providers carry. Every\nmodule builds and its tests pass.\n\n* rebase\n\n* Run the infrastructure provider e2e init as the provider, not as admin\n\nThe suite bootstrapped with kcp''s admin kubeconfig, and on this branch''s\nkcp that is refused:\n\n  init: provider workspace bootstrap: install: apply APIExport: creating\n  APIExport infrastructure.providers.railgrid.ai is forbidden:\n  spec.resources[0].group: Forbidden: API group\n  \"infrastructure.railgrid.ai\" is reserved by PermissionClaimPolicy\n  \"railgrid\"; only its providers may export it\n\nThe platform''s PermissionClaimPolicy reserves every API group it names and\nlists the subjects that may export one, and kcp-admin is not among them.\nmain never hit this because main binds upstream kcp v0.33.0, which has no\nsuch admission.\n\nMint the provider ServiceAccount''s kubeconfig from the provider-token\nSecret the hub''s Provider controller publishes, and hand init that. It is\nwhat `make init-provider-infrastructure` and the chart''s init container\nalready do, and what every other provider suite already does; this suite\nwas the only one reading no provider-token at all.\n\n* Treat an unresolved admin virtual workspace as an absent API\n\nThe External KCP suite deploys the hub against a released kcp image\n(ghcr.io/kcp-dev/kcp:v0.33.0, pinned in create_kcp.go), while the hub\nbinary is built from the vendored fork. The hub crash-looped there for 45\nminutes until Helm''s wait expired:\n\n  Error: installing PermissionClaimPolicy: discovering admin.kcp.io/v1alpha1:\n  forbidden: User \"railgrid-e2e-admin\" cannot get path\n  \"/services/admin/clusters/root/apis/admin.kcp.io/v1alpha1\":\n  Path not resolved to a valid virtual workspace\n\nPermissionClaimPolicyServed is meant to make this a no-op — its comment\nsays one info log on every kcp that does not serve admin.kcp.io, which is\ntoday''s — and it tolerated NotFound and a group-discovery failure. But the\ngroup lives behind a virtual workspace, and a path no virtual workspace\nclaims comes back 403: the framework''s authorizer has no opinion on a path\nit cannot resolve, and no opinion becomes a denial. So absence arrives as\nForbidden, which was neither tolerated case, and the caller treats a\nreturned error as fatal.\n\nTolerate that one shape, matched on the reason rather than on Forbidden\nalone: a denial from a kcp that does serve the group is a credential\nproblem, and reading it as absence would silently skip installing a policy\nwhose job is to reserve API groups.\n\n* Revendor kcp with the subresource path fix\n\nmjudeikis/kcp 20f1d300c. The APIExport virtual workspace now forwards the\npath a client addressed below a custom subresource, instead of carrying\nover only the query.\n\nThat is what the kuery edge aggregation needed. kuery reaches an edge''s\nKubernetes API through the k8s verb on kubernetesclusters, so every request\nis \"<edge>/k8s/<kube path>\". Inbound the virtual workspace saw\n.../kuery-k8s/k8s/apis/apiextensions.k8s.io/v1/customresourcedefinitions;\noutbound it sent .../kubernetesclusters:<identity>/kuery-k8s/k8s?watch=true.\nWith no tail the edges provider resolves /k8s/, the agent proxies the edge\napiserver''s root, and the root answers 200 with its paths document -- so\nclient-go reported \"Object ''Kind'' is missing\" and the CRD informer never\nsynced. Nothing else caught it because every other path through the k8s\nverb goes via the shard, which preserves the inbound path.\n\nAll twelve pins move together. Every module builds and its tests pass.\n\n* Report why a provider e2e bootstrap failed, in every suite\n\nThe kuery provider suite failed with\n\n  kuery init failed: exit status 1 (log: /tmp/railgrid-e2e-kuery-.../init.log)\n\nand that directory is removed by cleanup and never uploaded, so the reason\nwas gone. The infrastructure suite had the same blindness and it cost three\nCI rounds to read one admission error.\n\nTail the log to stderr, taken before cleanup, in the four suites that still\nhid it: edges, edgesconn, kueryprovider and provider.\n\n* Tell the kuery provider e2e where its data plane is\n\n  init: provider workspace bootstrap: install: DataPlaneEndpointSlice: no\n  data-plane base URL (set Options.DataPlaneURL, or point\n  Options.CatalogEntryFile at a CatalogEntry with spec.serving.backend.url)\n\nkuery''s APIExport declares \"<resource>/<verb>\" entries, so init writes the\nDataPlaneEndpointSlice they resolve through, and it refuses to guess the\naddress. The suite deliberately gave init no CatalogEntry, because it\nregisters the entry itself.\n\nPass the address directly, as the quickstart and infrastructure suites do.\nIt is the one applyKueryManifests already rewrites spec.serving.backend.url\nto, so the slice and the entry agree on where the provider is; reading the\ncommitted manifest instead would record the dev-loop port.\n\n* Report what the provider said when the edge-proxy gate refuses\n\nTestDEdgeProxyAuthBoundary fails with\n\n  wait timeout after 30s; last status: status=404 (want 502)\n\nand 404 is what the gate answers for every refusal, deliberately: a denial\nmust not disclose whether the addressed edge exists. So the status cannot\nsay whether the caller was denied, the object was unreadable as the\nprovider, or the review never ran, and the three have different causes.\n\nKeep the provider log path in suite state and dump its tail when the probe\nnever reaches 502.\n\n* Name the hop that refused the edge-proxy probe\n\nThe provider log tail showed only startup, because one line in these logs can\nbe a whole API object, so forty lines cover a few milliseconds. The useful\nfact was in what was absent: nothing in the provider log mentions the edge\nthe probe addresses, so the 404 comes from before the provider and the gate\nis not what refused.\n\nMatch on the edge name instead of tailing, over the hub''s log as well as the\nprovider''s, so the next run says which hop answered.\n\n* Forward a ServiceAccount request to the workspace it addresses\n\nTestDEdgeProxyAuthBoundary read 404 where it wanted 502, before and after\nthe grant, and nothing in the edges provider''s log ever mentioned the edge\nthe probe addresses: the request never got past kcp. The hub''s proxy was\nsending it to a path that cannot exist.\n\n  inbound    /clusters/{tenant}/apis/edges.railgrid.ai/v1alpha1/\n             kubernetesclusters/e2e-engage/k8s/api\n  forwarded  /clusters/{sa}/clusters/{tenant}/apis/...\n\nserveServiceAccount resolves the workspace from the token''s clusterName\nclaim, which is where the ServiceAccount LIVES, not where the request is\naimed. It de-doubled the prefix only when the path named the SA''s own\ncluster, so a request addressing another workspace -- which a data-plane\nverb on another provider''s object always does -- got a second prefix\nprepended. main never hit this because the probe took the old grammar,\nwhich carried no cluster prefix.\n\nHonour a cluster named in the path and forward it unchanged. That is safe\nbecause the SA token travels as it arrived: kcp authenticates it natively\nand authorizes it in THAT workspace, so reaching another one still needs a\ngrant there, which is what this test grants. Both checks the claim gets --\nthe traversal regex and the O-10 Org refusal -- are applied to the\naddressed cluster too, so naming one in the path walks around neither. The\nagent-kubeconfig case the old strip existed for falls out of the same\nbranch.","tree":{"sha":"93e5d8fe8fe90486324b943611a8ae712d94a052","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/railgrid/git/trees/93e5d8fe8fe90486324b943611a8ae712d94a052"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/railgrid/git/commits/76b9080bce003fec7a1d8b66324373a791842149","verification":{"payload":"tree 93e5d8fe8fe90486324b943611a8ae712d94a052\nparent 70fbb08d08167c0997278b0682abe3ef25ff920e\nauthor Mangirdas Judeikis <mangirdas@judeikis.lt> 1790411831 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790411831 +0300\n\nAdjust providers machinery (#777)\n\n* Fix nerw provider struct\n\n* Adjust providers logic\n\n* Move to better providers machinery\n\n* move provider contract\n\n* fix CI\n\n* Claim the kind a required verb coordinate hangs off\n\nkcp refuses an APIExport whose permission claim names a custom\nsubresource without a claim on the resource that serves it. App Studio\nrequired code.railgrid.ai connections/mint-registry-token but never\nconnections, so `init` died at admission:\n\n  spec.permissionClaims[13].resource: Invalid value:\n  \"connections/mint-registry-token\": a claim on a custom subresource\n  requires a claim on \"connections\", which is what serves it\n\nApp Studio does read those Connections — it lists them when a project\npicks one and gets one back when a view resolves its connectionRef — so\nthe claim was simply missing, not deliberately absent. Declare it\nread-only and regenerate the export.\n\nValidateProviderRequirement now rejects a verb coordinate whose parent is\nnot declared in the same requirement, in either order, so the next one\nfails in `make verify` with the coordinate named rather than at a\nprovider''s own bootstrap.\n\nThe infrastructure provider e2e reported \"provider init failed: exit\nstatus 1\" and left the reason in a temp file CI does not upload; tail it\nto stderr.\n\nAgents ran with the WRONG identity: run-provider-agents resolved\nRAILGRID_PROVIDER_KUBECONFIG by taking the first file that existed out of\nthe provider''s own kubeconfig, kcp''s admin kubeconfig and the Tilt front\nproxy''s. Started before `agents-init` mints the first, it silently bound\nto kcp-admin, which authenticates but holds nothing in the provider\nworkspace, so every data-plane verb failed the gate:\n\n  dataplane: reading APIExportEndpointSlice agents.railgrid.ai:\n  Unauthorized\n\nserving HTTP 500 to the portal''s model discovery, and the controllers\nlogged Unauthorized lease errors on a loop. A verb runs as the provider,\nso no other identity will do: use the provider''s own kubeconfig or refuse\nto start, and watch the file in Tilt so init''s write restarts serve —\nwhat kuery and infrastructure already do.\n\n* Accept every requirement kuery declares when enabling it\n\nTestKueryAggregatesEdgeObjects failed with kuery unable to discover the\nedge''s Kubernetes API:\n\n  kuery engage: discovery failed for cluster 1vqly0gm36vbqs0b/kuery-k8s:\n  unknown\n\nThe edges provider never saw the request. kcp refused it first:\n\n  authorization step reason=\"failed to find suitable reason to allow\n  access in APIBinding\" decision=\"Denied\" resource=\"kubernetesclusters\"\n  subresource=\"k8s\" verb=\"get\" ... resp=403\n\nThe tenant''s APIBinding asked for three claims and accepted one. Its\nexportPermissionClaims carried kubernetesclusters, kubernetesclusters/k8s\nand subjectaccessreviews; appliedPermissionClaims carried only\nkubernetesclusters, because the test''s Enable ticked that one kind by\nname. A claim the binding does not accept is a coordinate the virtual\nworkspace does not serve, so the k8s verb was never reachable and the\ndiscovery client rendered the 403 as \"unknown\".\n\nRead the declaration from the hub catalog and accept all of it, which is\nwhat the portal and `railgrid dev` already do. A hardcoded list stops\nmatching spec.requires the moment a requirement is added, which is how\nthis test came to enable a kind without the verb on it.\n\n* Read the e2e bootstrap logs before cleanup deletes them\n\nThe tail added for the infrastructure provider e2e ran after cleanup(),\nwhich removes the whole data dir, so the failure reported\n\n  provider init failed: exit status 1 (log: /tmp/.../init.log)\n  (open /tmp/.../init.log: no such file or directory)\n\nTake the tail first. Give the serve readiness check the same treatment:\nits log is in the same directory and would be just as gone.\n\n* Claim the infrastructure Templates App Studio reads\n\nProject details failed with:\n\n  templates.infrastructure.railgrid.ai is forbidden: User\n  \"system:serviceaccount:default:provider\" cannot list resource\n  \"templates\" in API group \"infrastructure.railgrid.ai\" at the cluster\n  scope: access denied\n\nApp Studio reads that catalog in three places — preparing a project''s\ndetail view, resolving the template a binding names, and the assistant''s\ntemplate tool — but spec.requires named only instances and its verb\ncoordinates, so the generated claim never covered the kind and kcp served\nnothing for it. Declare it read-only.\n\nAudited every provider for the same class both ways a GVR is spelled in\nthis tree, the composite literal and GroupVersion.WithResource: no other\nkind is read from a group its provider does not declare. The one other\ncross-group read, agents reading the workspace''s MCPServer, is deliberate\n— it runs as the CALLER, on their bearer, so it needs no claim.\n\n* Revendor kcp at the custom-subresource parent gate\n\nmjudeikis/kcp 3b4bd7a70 (\"fix getter\"), one commit on from ba9e0a6a4.\nIt adds gateOnParent to the APIExport virtual workspace''s custom\nsubresource storage: before a verb runs, the object it hangs off is read\nback through the storage that serves the parent, label selector and all,\nso a claimer cannot reach a subresource of an object its claim does not\ncover.\n\nThat makes the parent claim load-bearing at request time rather than only\nat APIExport admission. Every verb coordinate this repo declares already\nsits under a claimed parent, including the code.railgrid.ai/connections\none added earlier in this branch, so the gate has an object to read in\neach case.\n\nAll twelve pins move together: the six replace targets in the root module\nplus the apimachinery/v2 pin the SDK and four providers carry. Every\nmodule builds and its tests pass.\n\n* rebase\n\n* Run the infrastructure provider e2e init as the provider, not as admin\n\nThe suite bootstrapped with kcp''s admin kubeconfig, and on this branch''s\nkcp that is refused:\n\n  init: provider workspace bootstrap: install: apply APIExport: creating\n  APIExport infrastructure.providers.railgrid.ai is forbidden:\n  spec.resources[0].group: Forbidden: API group\n  \"infrastructure.railgrid.ai\" is reserved by PermissionClaimPolicy\n  \"railgrid\"; only its providers may export it\n\nThe platform''s PermissionClaimPolicy reserves every API group it names and\nlists the subjects that may export one, and kcp-admin is not among them.\nmain never hit this because main binds upstream kcp v0.33.0, which has no\nsuch admission.\n\nMint the provider ServiceAccount''s kubeconfig from the provider-token\nSecret the hub''s Provider controller publishes, and hand init that. It is\nwhat `make init-provider-infrastructure` and the chart''s init container\nalready do, and what every other provider suite already does; this suite\nwas the only one reading no provider-token at all.\n\n* Treat an unresolved admin virtual workspace as an absent API\n\nThe External KCP suite deploys the hub against a released kcp image\n(ghcr.io/kcp-dev/kcp:v0.33.0, pinned in create_kcp.go), while the hub\nbinary is built from the vendored fork. The hub crash-looped there for 45\nminutes until Helm''s wait expired:\n\n  Error: installing PermissionClaimPolicy: discovering admin.kcp.io/v1alpha1:\n  forbidden: User \"railgrid-e2e-admin\" cannot get path\n  \"/services/admin/clusters/root/apis/admin.kcp.io/v1alpha1\":\n  Path not resolved to a valid virtual workspace\n\nPermissionClaimPolicyServed is meant to make this a no-op — its comment\nsays one info log on every kcp that does not serve admin.kcp.io, which is\ntoday''s — and it tolerated NotFound and a group-discovery failure. But the\ngroup lives behind a virtual workspace, and a path no virtual workspace\nclaims comes back 403: the framework''s authorizer has no opinion on a path\nit cannot resolve, and no opinion becomes a denial. So absence arrives as\nForbidden, which was neither tolerated case, and the caller treats a\nreturned error as fatal.\n\nTolerate that one shape, matched on the reason rather than on Forbidden\nalone: a denial from a kcp that does serve the group is a credential\nproblem, and reading it as absence would silently skip installing a policy\nwhose job is to reserve API groups.\n\n* Revendor kcp with the subresource path fix\n\nmjudeikis/kcp 20f1d300c. The APIExport virtual workspace now forwards the\npath a client addressed below a custom subresource, instead of carrying\nover only the query.\n\nThat is what the kuery edge aggregation needed. kuery reaches an edge''s\nKubernetes API through the k8s verb on kubernetesclusters, so every request\nis \"<edge>/k8s/<kube path>\". Inbound the virtual workspace saw\n.../kuery-k8s/k8s/apis/apiextensions.k8s.io/v1/customresourcedefinitions;\noutbound it sent .../kubernetesclusters:<identity>/kuery-k8s/k8s?watch=true.\nWith no tail the edges provider resolves /k8s/, the agent proxies the edge\napiserver''s root, and the root answers 200 with its paths document -- so\nclient-go reported \"Object ''Kind'' is missing\" and the CRD informer never\nsynced. Nothing else caught it because every other path through the k8s\nverb goes via the shard, which preserves the inbound path.\n\nAll twelve pins move together. Every module builds and its tests pass.\n\n* Report why a provider e2e bootstrap failed, in every suite\n\nThe kuery provider suite failed with\n\n  kuery init failed: exit status 1 (log: /tmp/railgrid-e2e-kuery-.../init.log)\n\nand that directory is removed by cleanup and never uploaded, so the reason\nwas gone. The infrastructure suite had the same blindness and it cost three\nCI rounds to read one admission error.\n\nTail the log to stderr, taken before cleanup, in the four suites that still\nhid it: edges, edgesconn, kueryprovider and provider.\n\n* Tell the kuery provider e2e where its data plane is\n\n  init: provider workspace bootstrap: install: DataPlaneEndpointSlice: no\n  data-plane base URL (set Options.DataPlaneURL, or point\n  Options.CatalogEntryFile at a CatalogEntry with spec.serving.backend.url)\n\nkuery''s APIExport declares \"<resource>/<verb>\" entries, so init writes the\nDataPlaneEndpointSlice they resolve through, and it refuses to guess the\naddress. The suite deliberately gave init no CatalogEntry, because it\nregisters the entry itself.\n\nPass the address directly, as the quickstart and infrastructure suites do.\nIt is the one applyKueryManifests already rewrites spec.serving.backend.url\nto, so the slice and the entry agree on where the provider is; reading the\ncommitted manifest instead would record the dev-loop port.\n\n* Report what the provider said when the edge-proxy gate refuses\n\nTestDEdgeProxyAuthBoundary fails with\n\n  wait timeout after 30s; last status: status=404 (want 502)\n\nand 404 is what the gate answers for every refusal, deliberately: a denial\nmust not disclose whether the addressed edge exists. So the status cannot\nsay whether the caller was denied, the object was unreadable as the\nprovider, or the review never ran, and the three have different causes.\n\nKeep the provider log path in suite state and dump its tail when the probe\nnever reaches 502.\n\n* Name the hop that refused the edge-proxy probe\n\nThe provider log tail showed only startup, because one line in these logs can\nbe a whole API object, so forty lines cover a few milliseconds. The useful\nfact was in what was absent: nothing in the provider log mentions the edge\nthe probe addresses, so the 404 comes from before the provider and the gate\nis not what refused.\n\nMatch on the edge name instead of tailing, over the hub''s log as well as the\nprovider''s, so the next run says which hop answered.\n\n* Forward a ServiceAccount request to the workspace it addresses\n\nTestDEdgeProxyAuthBoundary read 404 where it wanted 502, before and after\nthe grant, and nothing in the edges provider''s log ever mentioned the edge\nthe probe addresses: the request never got past kcp. The hub''s proxy was\nsending it to a path that cannot exist.\n\n  inbound    /clusters/{tenant}/apis/edges.railgrid.ai/v1alpha1/\n             kubernetesclusters/e2e-engage/k8s/api\n  forwarded  /clusters/{sa}/clusters/{tenant}/apis/...\n\nserveServiceAccount resolves the workspace from the token''s clusterName\nclaim, which is where the ServiceAccount LIVES, not where the request is\naimed. It de-doubled the prefix only when the path named the SA''s own\ncluster, so a request addressing another workspace -- which a data-plane\nverb on another provider''s object always does -- got a second prefix\nprepended. main never hit this because the probe took the old grammar,\nwhich carried no cluster prefix.\n\nHonour a cluster named in the path and forward it unchanged. That is safe\nbecause the SA token travels as it arrived: kcp authenticates it natively\nand authorizes it in THAT workspace, so reaching another one still needs a\ngrant there, which is what this test grants. Both checks the claim gets --\nthe traversal regex and the O-10 Org refusal -- are applied to the\naddressed cluster too, so naming one in the path walks around neither. The\nagent-kubeconfig case the old strip existed for falls out of the same\nbranch.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqt4Q3CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA4RkQAIMTRtDJtMjTu1VTJoSyk3mK\nFmrAvENQzG+uigTzP4MwXgy9PRcnBvMyxEpm5tp8c8mixQHeD7hw44PUJgbiU9j/\nyxFbvaGFUq/4/w4+GkpVbpa8pp/4mTFr81Ah91GA++AbmDDYd8U9VtL1k4jUAybb\njTeKrO/0kRFYXKvajxrEGa0Tf8Mh0cwcEtacc3jFtDy7MAXYPui1b8nqQOoMRUnE\nXGTxh1L4Kq24BnD/Iy5ILcIsmH0Xl85Zw4I/fWZpjqZpkdt9tGq1KK21on79ydi8\nF+Ndel83z4hk9xcwC1Xd1g5fv2fJ0BerjMw9Siypdk/x7cn/r+J7dL7RibZpxtj6\nLyPUU7P6FUzyXxOuSsLB0/nwsCY208JJSNCBZFWwQ31SeUnEgPXvzrOguL4LHBAs\njyXHZzRtISuOts7V1OHKln822mLTYQCq9x7odAufZY+4hAlu3gatMg24hrVBY4nT\nGEQkJ05BYTf9Scf/KRKF/9+zwXaSjZXB9CSvt4w3zVnFovWNpIsaUhK5u69yLcpV\nkgCPRg6dRyUQM3X87reVmx93Iy/RIvL4Jd9LVxyJoAkESwKPBByCSynAnEypKRpf\nzwqgLwKM0h7PjVE8becE3qUe3EKIdVaR3b8Vzd6Dgaky7LO9kzKFLB9cHjBG01bJ\nBsosVHYj0u7rIu4EaHfk\n=kuap\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-26T08:37:11Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/railgrid/railgrid/commit/76b9080bce003fec7a1d8b66324373a791842149","node_id":"C_kwDORSHIc9oAKDc2YjkwODBiY2UwMDNmZWM3YTFkOGI2NjMyNDM3M2E3OTE4NDIxNDk","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/railgrid/railgrid/commit/70fbb08d08167c0997278b0682abe3ef25ff920e","sha":"70fbb08d08167c0997278b0682abe3ef25ff920e","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/railgrid/commits/70fbb08d08167c0997278b0682abe3ef25ff920e"}],"sha":"76b9080bce003fec7a1d8b66324373a791842149","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/railgrid/commits/76b9080bce003fec7a1d8b66324373a791842149"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/5685031?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cwilhit/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cwilhit/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cwilhit/following{