ers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/railgrid/railgrid/commit/d002fd91629ab4f6bbd93a4d6e0758f3e84d4758","node_id":"C_kwDORSHIc9oAKGQwMDJmZDkxNjI5YWI0ZjZiYmQ5M2E0ZDZlMDc1OGYzZTg0ZDQ3NTg","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/railgrid/railgrid/commit/ed2d6c6a3019d24c7a5fd496b815295730492fc1","sha":"ed2d6c6a3019d24c7a5fd496b815295730492fc1","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/railgrid/commits/ed2d6c6a3019d24c7a5fd496b815295730492fc1"}],"sha":"d002fd91629ab4f6bbd93a4d6e0758f3e84d4758","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/railgrid/commits/d002fd91629ab4f6bbd93a4d6e0758f3e84d4758"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/170962632?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/antcybersec/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/antcybersec/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/antcybersec/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/antcybersec/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/antcybersec","id":170962632,"login":"antcybersec","node_id":"U_kgDOCjCuyA","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/antcybersec/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/antcybersec/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/antcybersec/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/antcybersec/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/antcybersec/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/antcybersec","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/railgrid/commits/ed2d6c6a3019d24c7a5fd496b815295730492fc1/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-17T06:41:39Z","email":"anant1234466@gmail.com","name":"anant"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-17T06:41:39Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"test(e2e): add kuery provider e2e suite (#761)\n\n* test: add kuery provider e2e suite (#409)\n\nThree of the eight providers carry an e2e suite today (infrastructure,\nquickstart, edges); kuery, code, agents and app-studio have none. kuery is\nthe smallest of those and its shape maps onto the existing subprocess\nharness, so it is the cheapest to cover properly first.\n\nAdds test/e2e/suites/kueryprovider, modelled on test/e2e/suites/provider:\nrailgrid-hub with embedded kcp plus kuery-provider as host subprocesses, no\nkind/Helm/Dex. Covers provisioning (Provider + CatalogEntry, edgeProxyAccess,\nthe APIExport''s SavedView schema and four built-in permissionClaims, the\nunset maximalPermissionPolicy, the bind grant), the /api/providers DTO, the\nhub backend proxy, tenant Enable/Disable with a SavedView round-trip, and the\nquery surfaces that answer without a connected edge.\n\n/api/edges gets its own identity-contract test: absent identity is 401, a\nworkspace path in X-Railgrid-Tenant is 400, and a valid cluster ID returns\nthat tenant''s own empty fleet. That 400/401 split is a tenant-isolation\nboundary worth pinning.\n\nFleet queries over real objects need the engagement controller syncing\nthrough the edges-proxy, which needs the kind-based harness the edgesconn\nsuite uses; /api/query is exercised only on its empty-fleet behaviour here.\n\nWired as `make e2e-kuery-provider` and added to the e2e-providers CI job.\nPorts follow the existing +10 convention (19493/16493/18118) and, like the\nsibling suites, share the embedded-kcp etcd port 2380.\n\n* test: prove kuery aggregates objects from a live edge (#409)\n\nThe kueryprovider suite covers kuery''s provider plumbing but never sees real\ndata: its query surfaces are exercised against an empty fleet, so nothing\nasserts that kuery actually aggregates. Per review on #761, that is the half\nthat matters.\n\nAggregation only happens through the edges consumer proxy, which needs an\nengaged edge with a real cluster behind it. edgesconn already stands up a kind\ncluster, registers a KubernetesCluster, runs a live agent through the reverse\ntunnel and writes the edge-proxy grant, so this adds kuery to that suite\nrather than duplicating the harness.\n\nTestKueryAggregatesEdgeObjects engages an edge, creates a Deployment on the\nedge cluster, brings kuery up beside the edges provider, binds it into the\ntenant workspace, then waits for the engagement controller to pick the edge up\nand asserts that POST /api/query:\n\n  - returns the Deployment created on the edge,\n  - attributes it to the engaged edge''s cluster, and\n  - expands descendants+ down to the ReplicaSet and the Pod.\n\nThe relation walk is the real assertion. Returning the Deployment only proves\nthe sync loop copied an object; resolving Deployment -> ReplicaSet -> Pod\nproves kuery built the relationship graph a plain list cannot answer.\n\nkuery serves on :18099 beside edges on :18098, with a scratch sqlite store so\nthe suite never writes kuery.db into the repo. The connectivity target now\nbuilds kuery-provider and guards the extra port.\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: anant <170962632+antcybersec@users.noreply.github.com>","tree":{"sha":"32e8de77fc59388ea07441191bb7bf538ab14748","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/railgrid/git/trees/32e8de77fc59388ea07441191bb7bf538ab14748"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/railgrid/git/commits/ed2d6c6a3019d24c7a5fd496b815295730492fc1","verification":{"payload":"tree 32e8de77fc59388ea07441191bb7bf538ab14748\nparent 9cfcd07bfd62dd9b4650fbdc0ff4fa2714b76c28\nauthor anant <anant1234466@gmail.com> 1789627299 +0530\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789627299 +0300\n\ntest(e2e): add kuery provider e2e suite (#761)\n\n* test: add kuery provider e2e suite (#409)\n\nThree of the eight providers carry an e2e suite today (infrastructure,\nquickstart, edges); kuery, code, agents and app-studio have none. kuery is\nthe smallest of those and its shape maps onto the existing subprocess\nharness, so it is the cheapest to cover properly first.\n\nAdds test/e2e/suites/kueryprovider, modelled on test/e2e/suites/provider:\nrailgrid-hub with embedded kcp plus kuery-provider as host subprocesses, no\nkind/Helm/Dex. Covers provisioning (Provider + CatalogEntry, edgeProxyAccess,\nthe APIExport''s SavedView schema and four built-in permissionClaims, the\nunset maximalPermissionPolicy, the bind grant), the /api/providers DTO, the\nhub backend proxy, tenant Enable/Disable with a SavedView round-trip, and the\nquery surfaces that answer without a connected edge.\n\n/api/edges gets its own identity-contract test: absent identity is 401, a\nworkspace path in X-Railgrid-Tenant is 400, and a valid cluster ID returns\nthat tenant''s own empty fleet. That 400/401 split is a tenant-isolation\nboundary worth pinning.\n\nFleet queries over real objects need the engagement controller syncing\nthrough the edges-proxy, which needs the kind-based harness the edgesconn\nsuite uses; /api/query is exercised only on its empty-fleet behaviour here.\n\nWired as `make e2e-kuery-provider` and added to the e2e-providers CI job.\nPorts follow the existing +10 convention (19493/16493/18118) and, like the\nsibling suites, share the embedded-kcp etcd port 2380.\n\n* test: prove kuery aggregates objects from a live edge (#409)\n\nThe kueryprovider suite covers kuery''s provider plumbing but never sees real\ndata: its query surfaces are exercised against an empty fleet, so nothing\nasserts that kuery actually aggregates. Per review on #761, that is the half\nthat matters.\n\nAggregation only happens through the edges consumer proxy, which needs an\nengaged edge with a real cluster behind it. edgesconn already stands up a kind\ncluster, registers a KubernetesCluster, runs a live agent through the reverse\ntunnel and writes the edge-proxy grant, so this adds kuery to that suite\nrather than duplicating the harness.\n\nTestKueryAggregatesEdgeObjects engages an edge, creates a Deployment on the\nedge cluster, brings kuery up beside the edges provider, binds it into the\ntenant workspace, then waits for the engagement controller to pick the edge up\nand asserts that POST /api/query:\n\n  - returns the Deployment created on the edge,\n  - attributes it to the engaged edge''s cluster, and\n  - expands descendants+ down to the ReplicaSet and the Pod.\n\nThe relation walk is the real assertion. Returning the Deployment only proves\nthe sync loop copied an object; resolving Deployment -> ReplicaSet -> Pod\nproves kuery built the relationship graph a plain list cannot answer.\n\nkuery serves on :18099 beside edges on :18098, with a scratch sqlite store so\nthe suite never writes kuery.db into the repo. The connectivity target now\nbuilds kuery-provider and guards the extra port.\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: anant <170962632+antcybersec@users.noreply.github.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqq4ujCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAALpcQAJCGoaQuC3bhZNAulGkcx9Ed\n16YKpkIsdDNJRIAF/Aj1FT+8fX5zdfoDFFZL/JoM7B8Tooc84S4b3EzVPYdgw7gx\nm2x23Oupl0XSHDRq/EQ0zpWSBewSI+V7zzTFqRmpsxrT8oE0a4780hj+1lKw70KJ\nzuOOcJufiVTpLu05IRH6J8kRchGZDOgs4cpuz9pJfSkwkof6jNrio2b4jgH1OeY2\na9OMjhbMDeY8uaaouHlj5sQYJtQg1GyInQhCiA6gZcEN46pBmnnfu9bHuCm73JAs\nmHHwlRqyDvgY06u80wG0xRN6FX5HULSP2I2NBgi6tbcnThKyp0WO